  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-76893",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
